{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, d2kq5a_ (PthA), d3v6pa_ (dHax3), and d4cj9a_ (BurrH) all have the same lineage: all-alpha class a, left-handed alpha-alpha-superhelix fold a.298, TAL-effector-like superfamily a.298.1, and TAL-effector family a.298.1.1 (family SUNID 267609). The shared family—the deepest relevant homology-bearing level—supports a close relationship despite their different total lengths.

The explanation is repeat-number variation. SCOPe marks this as an `re` case: the common domain is composed of different numbers of small repeating units. Its family record identifies 4cj9 A:259-292 as the canonical representative repeat; those coordinates apply to d4cj9a_, not to the other domains. The original SEQRES domain sequences are 51 residues for d2kq5a_, 373 for d3v6pa_, and 752 for d4cj9a_. d2kq5a_ is explicitly annotated “single repeat,” whereas the longer sequences contain extended repeat arrays. Thus, SCOPe groups them by the homologous repeat module and shared repeat architecture, not by requiring equal array length.

This placement does not establish identical repeat counts, DNA-binding specificity, biochemical activity, whole-protein function, or interchangeable full-length templates. In particular, the 51-residue single-repeat structure may represent a local repeat core but cannot automatically model the complete 373- or 752-residue arrays. Stronger transfer would require unit-by-unit sequence alignment, structural superposition of corresponding repeats, matched domain/terminal context, target-bound structural evidence, biochemical specificity measurements, and—where evolutionary history is claimed—phylogenetic or literature evidence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d2kq5a_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3v6pa_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4cj9a_/homology","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d2kq5a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=original&format=json","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d3v6pa_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=original&format=json","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d4cj9a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=original&format=json","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267609","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267609/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267609/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267609/homology"]}]}
